Sure!  
Below is the exact invocation you’d use to serialize a `RootObjectMapper` to XContent while:

* **Including all default settings** (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date‑detection, numeric‑detection, …)  
* **Skipping runtime fields** (so they’re omitted from the output)

```java
// 1. Create an XContentBuilder instance (you already have it as `xContentBuilderInstance`)
//    – this is the builder you’ll pass to the function.

// 2. Build the ArrayList of parameters that control the serialization.
//    The first element tells the mapper to include default values.
//    The second element tells it to skip runtime fields.
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(true);   // includeDefaults
params.add(true);   // skipRuntimeFields

// 3. Call the helper function that does the actual XContent serialization.
Object result = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
    new Object[]{ 
        "xContentBuilderInstance",   // <-- string representation of your builder
        "[true, true]"                // <-- string representation of the ArrayList
    }
);
```

**What this does**

* The `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` helper will write the mapper’s JSON representation into `xContentBuilderInstance`.  
* Because `includeDefaults` is `true`, all default settings (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection, etc.) will be present in the output.  
* Because `skipRuntimeFields` is `true`, any runtime fields defined on the mapper will be omitted.

You can then inspect or use the `result` (which will be the same `xContentBuilderInstance` you passed in) to see the generated XContent.
